</section>

<section class="block" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head"><h2 id="about-h">How this worked</h2></div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="fig" data-k="st-starters">36</span><h3>Starter statements</h3><p data-k="st-starters-p">The conversation opened with 36 statements for people to agree or disagree with.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig" data-k="st-people">154</span><h3>People took part</h3><p data-k="st-people-p">Since 2 November they have voted agree, disagree or pass, and added statements of their own.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">3</span><h3>Groups emerged</h3><p>Polis, an open-source tool, found two groups of people who voted alike. One of them split clearly on several questions, so we show three. Nobody chose a group. We named them.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig" data-k="st-voters">54</span><h3>Voters set priorities</h3><p data-k="st-voters-p">On 2 November they spread a budget of credits across 13 possible changes, using quadratic voting.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <details class="fine">
      <summary>Notes for checking the numbers</summary>
      <div class="arows">
        <div class="arow"><h3>Reading the percentages</h3><p data-k="n-pct">Each figure is the share of a group who agreed, out of those in the group who voted on that statement. Later statements had fewer voters, so where fewer than 20 people in a group voted, we show how many. Group 3 has 15 people, so every figure for it shows how many voted. We apply the 7-in-10 bar before rounding, so a statement shown at 70% can still fall just short.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Reading the map</h3><p>We rebuilt the map from the published votes using the same method as Polis, so people sit close to where the Polis report places them, but not in exactly the same spots. Left to right is the main divide in the votes. Where many people sit close together, their dots pile up, so a dot can sit a little to one side of its exact place.</p><p>Each person’s card shows up to three of their votes as examples. For each group they sided with, it shows the statement most of that group agreed with (or rejected), judged cautiously when few people voted, preferring statements the person agreed with over ones they rejected. A statement that fits two groups is shown once.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>How we found three groups</h3><p>Polis, the tool that grouped people, found two groups. Inside the smaller one, people split clearly on several questions, so we show them as two groups. That makes three.</p><p data-k="n-inside">The 15 people at the far end of that group (Group 3) differ from the other 32 (Group 2) on five statements. On each, at least 65% agreed on one side and at most 35% on the other, with at least 10 people voting on each side, and a gap that big would happen by chance less than once in a thousand times.</p><p data-k="n-flips">Migration strengthening multiculturalism, innovation and international relationships: 8% of Group 3 agreed, against 86% of Group 2. Migration being essential to the healthcare workforce: 13% against 76%. Workers being able to walk away from a bad job without losing their visa: 7% against 67%. Migration’s role in growth, productivity and social development: 33% against 92%. A visa pathway for aged care and disability workers: 33% against 85%.</p><p data-k="n-chance">Trying many split points and statements can turn up a few differences by luck. So we also put the group’s members in 1,000 random orders and ran the same search on each (a permutation test). It never found more than two such statements, and in 805 of the 1,000 orders it found none. The real order shows five. The split isn’t down to people who mostly passed: without the three people in these two groups who passed on at least half their votes, three of the five statements still split them. Groups 2 and 3 voted on a median of 92 and 93 statements. Inside Group 1 the same test found only two such statements. Random orders sometimes find that many too, so we don’t split it.</p><p data-k="n-allies">We also looked for unlikely allies, people in different groups who back the same statements, and found none that held up.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The group cards</h3><p>Group 1’s and Group 3’s cards each show three statements most of the group agreed with and far fewer of both other groups did. Group 2 has no view that sets it apart from both neighbours, so its card shows where it sides with each, and one it backed more than both. RadicalxChange chose which statements to show. We chose statements about what each group wants, including what it values. Lines that mainly judge other people count in every figure, but aren’t on the cards.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Who and what we counted</h3><p data-k="n-stmts">195 statements were open for voting: the 36 starter statements and 159 written by people taking part. The moderators held back 98 others, and 2 more were still waiting for review, so no one else voted on them.</p><p data-k="n-people">We count the 154 people who voted on at least one of those 195 statements, the same as Polis’s own count. Between them they cast 11,409 votes.</p><p data-k="n-own">Three people’s only votes were the agreements Polis records when someone adds a statement. They count as taking part, but voted on too few statements to be placed in a group.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Pairing the statements</h3><p>RadicalxChange chose which statements to pair. Every number comes straight from the results.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The priority vote</h3><p data-k="n-qv">A separate vote with its own voters. We set aside ballots that used fewer than 10 credits (there were two, both empty). Where someone voted more than once, we counted the last ballot they submitted. That leaves 54 voters. Net votes are votes for minus votes against. The matching statements from the conversation are close in meaning, not identical in wording.</p><p data-k="n-chart">The chart shared after the session also shows 54 voters, but where someone voted more than once it kept the ballot that used the most credits, not the last one. So some totals differ by up to 9 votes, and the ten-year plan ties with the employer ban at +143. The same six changes come top either way.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The data</h3><p data-k="n-data">From the Polis export of 20 November 2026, 4:45am AEDT. The conversation is still open, so these results are preliminary. Statements appear exactly as people wrote them.</p></div>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
